// Permit a Read only if the same user logged in within the last hour and has not
// since logged out.
//
// The idiom is Dogwood's accepted "A but not B": a guarded negation restricts
// nothing, so it is legal only when a *preceding* conjunct in the same `&&`
// chain already range-restricts its free variables. Here the restrictor comes
// first, and the negation after it. Reversing the two is the rejected form, and
// Dogwood refuses to lower it.
//
// The event schema beside this file pins `callerPrincipal`, so every temporal
// predicate is automatically correlated to the principal of the request being
// decided: another user's Login is invisible to this rule, and there is nothing
// in the policy that has to say so.
@id("read_after_login")
permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::response{ input.user: context.input.user }
    && !Drupe::Action::"Logout"::response{ input.user: context.input.user }
};
